  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \nLess severe threat but still storm chances remain thru early\nnext week.\n\nFirst round of showers/storms this evening look to primarily\nimpact areas west of our region as the night progresses.\nHowever, those areas along and west of I-77 may receive a\nglancing blow from this activity. Given recent heavy rain,\nwe will be continuing the flood watch in this area. For the\nremainder of the flood watch area, confidence is not has high\nfor a strong impact. Likewise, chances are not zero. Some\nearlier solutions lean toward a late night/early Thursday \nmorning arrival of additional convective activity that may \nimpact other sections of the watch area. Given the above, our\nforecast will reflect the greatest potential for showers/storms\nto remain across areas east of I-77 for tonight's forecast, with\nlesser chances east. Little to no precipitation is still \nexpected east of the crest of the Blue Ridge.\n\nLess coverage Thursday with limited upper support based on \nmodel consensus, so just typical widely scattered activity \nmainly in the afternoon. Another wave does arrive later Thursday\nnight into Friday and probability of measurable precip is a \nlittle higher especially over the mountains of WV and adjacent \nAlleghanys of VA. Timing of this wave to that late in the night \nleads to overall lower severe threat. May have to watch for \nflooding again if storms train over the same area.\n\nThe overall pattern aloft remains the same but the ridge does\nbuild a little more northeastward by this weekend and nudge the\nmore organized convection further north. Another stronger\nshortwave kicks the ridge southward again by early next week so\ncould see another better chance of storm coverage. The flow\nappears to become more westerly aloft by Tue-Wed so fast moving\nsystems aloft with limited showers/thunderstorm coverage.\n\nTemperatures through the period will likely stay above normal \nexcept a brief drop to near normal this weekend.",
